Haiku: 7-Zip 26.03's POSIX sources assume Linux, a BSD, AIX, Solaris or QNX.

Four things in the console build do not exist on Haiku:

1. struct dirent has no d_type (it is {d_dev, d_pdev, d_ino, d_pino, d_reclen,
   d_name[]}).  CPP/Windows/FileFind.{h,cpp} read it for CDirEntry::Type,
   IsDots, NextAny and DirEntry_IsDir, and already compile those uses out on
   AIX, Solaris and QNX.  Add Haiku to that list.  The code behind the guard
   handles an unknown type with a stat() (Fill_FileInfo), so directory scans
   keep working.
2. <sys/sysinfo.h> (Linux only) is included by CPP/Windows/System.cpp.  Haiku
   has neither it nor <sys/sysctl.h>, but sysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES) works, so use
   the existing sysconf branch (Solaris') for GetRamSize.
3. <sys/sysmacros.h> and major()/minor() (CPP/7zip/Common/FileStreams.cpp,
   CPP/7zip/UI/Common/UpdateCallback.cpp), used to report a device node's
   numbers.  Haiku has neither, and st_rdev is documented as "not used", so
   both report 0.
   (CPP/Windows/FileFind.cpp has the include commented out.)

Every hunk is behind __HAIKU__, so no other platform compiles different code.
The sources use CRLF line endings; the patch keeps them.

Checked on a Haiku x86_64 VM (hrev57937): the console builds and links, and a
nested tree with an empty directory round-trips through `7zz a` / `7zz x`
byte for byte.

